    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Three-dimensional visualization tools aid in the assessment of stress and displacement fields around modeled faults. By viewing the fields in three-dimensions, it is possible to evaluate the spatial variability of quantities such as the maximum tensile stress or maximum Coulomb shear stresses. This information enables prediction of the three- dimensional location and orientation of secondary structures associated with slip on the fault, including joints and shear fractures, and the effect of fault-slip on neighboring faults. A simple example illustrates this approach.The work is motivated by a desire to understand the zigzag pattern of normal faulting characteristic of regions of crustal extension. Analysis of the stress field in three dimensions gives insight to the mechanical interaction among faults which is hypothesized to control the development of the zigzag fault pattern.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
